label week8_start:
    $ current_week = "Week 8"

    # ==============================================================================
    # ACT II - CHAPTER IV: A SATURDAY CONFESSION
    # ==============================================================================

    scene bg bedroom_late_afternoon
    with dissolve_scene_full

    n "The heavy hum of the evening fan in your boarding house room did little to clear the thick, looping thoughts running through your head."
    n "It had been an entire week since that suffocating afternoon in civics class, and the space between you and Trida had felt oddly hollow ever since."
    n "Even though you shared classes, they had masterfully dodged eye contact, slipped out of rooms before you could catch up, and kept their usual bright, frantic chatter locked tightly away behind a guarded, quiet smile."

    n "You were sitting on the edge of your bed, staring blankly at your phone screen as you replayed that awful silence over and over, when a sudden, sharp rapping echoed against your room door."

    play sound "audio/bump.opus"
    n "Frowning, you pushed yourself up and pulled the door open, expecting the building manager or a neighbour. Instead, your breath caught."

    show trida casual embarrassed at center with dissolve
    n "Standing in the dim hallway light was Trida. They looked a little worn out, and they offered a painfully hesitant, tight-lipped smile that didn't quite reach their eyes."
    n "Their hands were shoved deep into their front pocket, nervously fidgeting with the fabric."

    trida "Um... hey,"
    n "They started, their voice softer and more guarded than usual as they shifted their weight from one foot to the other."
    trida "Is this... is this a bad time? I can come back later if you're busy or anything."

    n "Instantly, a spike of worry cut through your surprise. Before they could even pull their hands away to retreat, you shook your head and stepped back, pulling the door wide open."
    p "No, it's not a bad time. Come on in."
    n "You said, your tone dropping any trace of hesitation as you gestured for them to come inside."

    hide trida with dissolve
    n "Trida blinked, clearly startled that you let them in so easily, but they stepped across the threshold with quiet, cautious movements."
    n "They didn't linger by the desk or pace around as they normally did; instead, they walked straight over to the small lounge area in your boarding house and sat on the edge of the couch, pulling their knees up slightly and looking entirely too small."

    show trida sad at center with dissolve
    n "The small lounge area felt entirely too quiet, the steady hum of the small wall-mounted air conditioner serving as the only background noise against the heavy, palpable tension in the air."
    n "Trida sat rigidly on the edge of the worn fabric couch, their posture tightly coiled and defensive."
    n "They had pulled their knees up slightly, arms wrapped defensively across the oversized fabric of their hoodie, fingers clutching at the opposite sleeves as if trying to hold themselves together physically."

    n "You sat down on the opposite end of the small sofa, leaving a respectful buffer of space so they wouldn't feel cornered, though you angled your body toward them."
    p "Relax, Trida, it's alright…"
    n "You said gently, when your voice came out soft and steady."
    p "You're sitting like a statue… Kick your shoes off, and just get comfortable."

    n "Trida let out a dry, self-deprecating laugh that sounded more like an exhausted sigh."
    n "They made a weak, half-hearted attempt to uncoil their posture, letting their feet drop to the floor, but their shoulders remained locked up near their ears."
    n "Their gaze bounced restlessly around your room—flickering over your desk, the posters on the wall, the edge of the rug—anything to avoid locking eyes with you directly for more than a split second."
    n "The nervous twitch in their fingers didn't stop; their thumbs kept nervously rubbing against the cuffs of their sleeves."

    trida "Yeah, sorry…"
    n "Trida murmured, making their voice sound brittle and entirely stripped of its usual brightness."
    trida "I guess... I guess old habits die hard. Sitting still is kind of terrifying when your brain won't shut off."

    n "You shifted closer, making the cushions dip slightly under your weight, bridging a bit of the distance between you."
    n "The worry pooling in your chest deepened, seeing how raw and fragile they still looked a full week after that civics class disaster."
    p "Trida,"
    n "You tried to keep your tone warm and completely devoid of any pressure."
    p "I know you didn't just show up here out of nowhere on a Saturday evening just to talk about nothing."
    p "You've been dodging me all week, and you've barely said two words…"
    p "What's actually going on in that head of yours? You can tell me."

    n "At the direct invitation to open up, the defensive scaffolding Trida had spent the last seven days desperately trying to construct suddenly fractured."
    n "Their breath caught in their throat, making a sharp hitch that visibly rattled their frame."
    n "Trida closed their eyes tightly for a brief moment, their jaws working as they swallowed hard against a sudden wave of emotion."
    n "When they opened them again, the restless bouncing of their gaze stopped, locking onto a random spot on the wall."

    n "A long, trembling sigh slipped past their lips. Their breath was visibly unsteady, shuddering softly in the quiet air of the room as the heavy weight of everything they had been holding back finally began to press down on them."
    n "Trida stared down at their hands, their thumbs anxiously digging into the fabric of their sleeves. When they finally spoke, the sound was barely more than a cracked whisper, stripped entirely of its usual confidence."

    trida "Every time I close my eyes, I just... I keep hearing what he said."
    n "They mumbled, their voice wavering violently. They let out a wet, shaky laugh, a pathetic sound meant to deflect, but the tears welling up in their eyes completely betrayed it."
    trida "It's stupid, right? It was just some throwaway comment from a guy who couldn't even care less about me. But... he was right."

    n "You shifted closer on the couch, with your chest tightening."
    p "Trida, he wasn't right. He was just being an asshole."

    show trida sad at center with dissolve
    trida "No, he {i}was{/i} right!"
    n "Trida suddenly snapped, their voice cracking sharply as the fragile barrier they'd built all week finally shattered."
    n "They turned to look at you, their eyes swimming with tears, the raw, unfiltered devastation on their face making it hard to breathe."
    trida "I {i}am{/i} dead weight! I mess up every basic thing, I can't even open a file or follow a simple instruction without turning into a total clown, and the second things get real, I just freeze up!"

    n "They pulled their knees up to their chest, burying their face against their arms for a second as a harsh, ragged sob hitched in their throat."
    n "When they lifted their head back up, their cheeks were wet, and their expression was twisted with crushing self-loathing."
    trida "Remember when I said I wanted to make people smile? Wanted to be someone who actually helped?"
    n "A bitter and broken laugh was slipping slowly like a downpour."
    trida "It's a joke. It's all just... me running away from how useless I feel. Before I moved here, I struggled with my art classes back home."
    trida "My teachers looked at me the same way—sighing, shaking their heads, and wondering why I even bothered trying when I had no natural talent."
    trida "I spent years telling myself, 'At least you're optimistic! At least you keep the mood light!' But what's the point?"

    n "Their voice dropped into a defeated whisper, the fight completely drained out of them."
    trida "When you're cheerful all the time, people just think you're stupid. And the second you crash out... you realise you don't actually have anything else."
    trida "I'm just loud, and clumsy, and completely incapable of doing anything right. Sometimes... honestly? Sometimes I seriously wonder why I even try. Why do I have such high hopes when every single time, I just end up proving everyone right about me?"

    n "The rawness of their confession hung heavy in the air, the silence of the room filled only by the soft, uneven sound of Trida's breathing."
    n "Without a second thought, you shifted closer on the couch, closing the remaining distance between you until your shoulder pressed firmly against theirs."
    n "You reached out, offering a grounding hand to grip their arm."

    p "Look at me, Trida."

    # Visual Splash Art: Trida Held Hands with Players
    $ splash_art = True
    n "Slowly your hands took Trida's fingers as both of your skin brushed against each other, making a little shudder through their hands."
    n "Your voice was firm with deep earnestness, and stripped of any hesitation."
    n "It took a moment, but Trida slowly raised their head, their tear-filled eyes blinking in surprise as they met your gaze."

    p "You are not useless."
    n "You said softly, letting every word carry absolute conviction."
    p "The way you talk about yourself... None of that is true. You think you're just a clown or a distraction, but you have no idea how much light you actually bring into a room."
    p "You make people smile, and you've made me so genuinely happy just by being around. Your optimism isn't a fake mask—it's who you are, and it's wonderful."
    p "You care so much about everyone else, and it kills me to see you beat yourself up over one miserable guy who doesn't even know you."

    n "You squeezed their arm gently, holding their gaze."
    p "You're talented, you're resilient, and you are so much more than a few bad moments in a classroom. I care about you, Trida. Please don't ever think you're alone in this, or that you aren't enough."

    n "The words hit Trida like a physical wave. For a second, they completely stopped breathing, their lips parting slightly as the profound weight of genuine, unconditional validation washed over them."
    n "They looked like someone standing out in the cold who had suddenly found a hearth fire. It had been so long—perhaps years—since anyone had spoken to them with such fierce, unreserved kindness, seeing past all their noise and catching them when they fell."
    n "A fresh swell of emotion hit them, but this time, the heavy, crushing despair began to dissolve, replaced by a sudden, overwhelming rush of warmth."

    # Visual Splash Art: Trida Blushing and Smiling to Players While Holding Hands
    show trida embarrassed at center with dissolve
    n "A watery chuckle slipped past Trida's lips, and despite the fresh tears still tracking down their flushed cheeks, a wide, genuine grin broke across their face—radiant, messy, and entirely disarming."
    n "Seeing that sudden, brilliant smile bloom right through their tears made your breath hitch, and your heart gave an unexpected, heavy thud against your ribs, skipping a beat entirely."

    n "Trida let out a shaky, half-laugh, scrubbing a messy sleeve across their face to wipe away the remaining tears while the wide grin stayed stubbornly plastered across their features."

    trida "Damn it... You're really not fair, you know that?"
    n "They whispered, their voice thick with emotion, but shining with a sudden, overwhelming lightness as they leaned into your shoulder."
    trida "Nobody... nobody's ever said stuff like that to me before. Thank you. Seriously... thank you."

    $ tridaAffection += 2
    $ splash_art = False

    n "You didn't let go of the contact, keeping your hand steady against their arm and rubbing a reassuring thumb soothingly against their sleeve."
    p "Are you actually, really... like really okay now?"
    n "You asked quietly, with a more protective register as you checked them over."
    p "Your hands are still freezing. Should I give you some water? Just take a deep breath."

    n "Trida let out another long breath, but this time, the tension visibly drained out of their rigid spine. The terrified edge in their eyes finally melted away and was replaced by warmth."
    n "They let their head lean fully against your shoulder, resting there with a sense of complete, unguarded trust that they rarely, if ever, showed to anyone else."

    n "For a few quiet moments, the only sound in the room was the steady hum of the air conditioner and the even rhythm of their breathing as it finally stabilised."

    show trida happy at center with dissolve
    n "Then, a faint, familiar spark flickered back to life behind their tear-damp lashes."
    n "Trida shifted slightly, letting out a small, self-deprecating snort as a hint of their classic, overly dramatic flair crept back into their voice."

    trida "Man... look at you,"
    n "They mumbled, a soft, teasing edge finally weaving its way back into their tone."
    trida "Practically turning into a certified motivational speaker on a Saturday night. What's next, are you going to start giving out inspirational quote posters at the door?"

    n "Even though their voice was a little raspy from crying, the teasing jab was unmistakably {i}them{/i}."
    n "It was the return of the Trida you knew—not hiding behind a loud, frantic mask this time, but sharing an intimate, quiet joke just between the two of you, sitting shoulder-to-shoulder in the dim light of your room."

    hide trida with dissolve
    scene black
    with dissolve_scene_full

    jump week9_start
